BE-300 2.4.18: enable networking, RTC, and clean up the INPUT block.
Plus the modern-GCC fixes that surface once these subsystems start
building.

Config changes (config_be300):
  * CONFIG_NET=y plus the netif options needed to match kernels/2.4.log:
    CONFIG_PACKET, CONFIG_NETLINK_DEV, CONFIG_UNIX, CONFIG_INET.
    Networking sub-features (filter, advanced routing, IPv6, IPX, etc.)
    stay off — only the basic banners "Linux NET4.0", "Initializing RT
    netlink socket", "NET4: Linux TCP/IP 1.0", "NET4: Unix domain
    sockets" need to fire for parity with the prebuilt kernel.
  * CONFIG_SYSVIPC=y, CONFIG_SYSCTL=y (most userspace expects these).
  * CONFIG_MIPS_RTC=y (the VR41xx internal RTC; "Generic MIPS RTC
    Driver v1.0" banner). Generic CONFIG_RTC stays off; that one is
    the ISA PC/AT RTC and irrelevant to MIPS.
  * Resolve the duplicated CONFIG_INPUT_* block at lines 547-553. The
    file had `CONFIG_INPUT_KEYBDEV=y` immediately followed by `#
    CONFIG_INPUT_KEYBDEV is not set` (and the same for EVDEV); oldconfig
    honored the first occurrence so /dev/input/event0 still worked, but
    the .config diff was self-contradictory. Drop the duplicate
    "is not set" overrides.

Modern-GCC fixes that come along for the ride once CONFIG_NET=y starts
building net/ and CONFIG_SYSCTL=y starts building kernel/sysctl.c:

  * kernel/sysctl.c: three sites use `((char *) buffer)++` to walk a
    `void __user *buffer`. A cast result is not an lvalue under strict
    C semantics; modern GCC rejects it. Same fix as fs/readdir.c in
    0001-modern-toolchain-fixes.patch.

  * include/net/ipv6.h, include/net/icmp.h: declare `extern struct
    ipv6_mib`, `icmpv6_mib`, `udp_mib`, `icmp_mib` arrays without first
    including <net/snmp.h>, which is where those structs live. Old
    GCC accepted incomplete-type arrays in declarations; modern GCC
    requires the size at point of declaration. Pull in <net/snmp.h>
    at the top of each header.

  * arch/mips/Makefile: 2.4.18 uses ~500 `extern __inline` definitions
    in include/net/{tcp,ip}.h with the classic GNU semantics (inline,
    do not emit standalone). C99 inverted those semantics and modern
    GCC follows C99 by default, so every TU emits its own copy of
    `between`, `IPCB`, `__skb_pull`, etc. and the linker rejects them
    as multiple-definition. Pass -fgnu89-inline to restore the old
    behavior across the kernel build.

